<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the Developer ID signature that .github/workflows/mac-build.yml
  gives the Mac app (hardened runtime, notarized). Deliberately empty.

  This matches what the Godot export already ships: the "macOS Private" preset
  keeps every codesign/entitlements/* option at its default (off), and its
  ad-hoc signed app has the hardened runtime flag with an empty entitlement
  dictionary. That app loads the island in CI and is the build the owner plays.

  Left out on purpose, each one a hardened-runtime exception the game does not use:
  - com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit / allow-unsigned-executable-memory: Godot needs
    them only for C# (.NET); GDScript runs in its own interpreter. Vulkan goes through
    MoltenVK, statically linked into the executable, which compiles shaders through
    Metal and writes no executable memory itself.
  - com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation: needed only to load libraries
    signed by another team. The app links only system frameworks; any GDExtension
    library added later sits inside the bundle and is signed with the same identity.
  - com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables: not used.
  - com.apple.security.device.audio-input / camera, and the app sandbox: not used.
  - com.apple.security.get-task-allow (debugging): the notary service rejects it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict/>
</plist>
